\cite[Def.~2.35]{adamek-rosicky}\label{def-accessibly-embedded}
Let $\mathcal{K}$ be an accessible category.
A  subcategory $\mathcal{B}\subseteq \mathcal{K}$ is said to be   \emph{accessibly embedded}  if it is full and there exists a regular cardinal $\lambda$ such that $\mathcal{B}$ is closed under $\lambda$-direct limits in $\mathcal{K}$. For any particular such $\lambda$, we will also say that $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{K}$ is  \emph{$\lambda$-accessibly embedded}.

\label{lemma-acc-embed}
Let $\lambda$ be a regular cardinal. Assume $\mathcal{K}$ is a $\lambda$-accessible category, and   $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{K}$ is a strictly full subcategory with $\mathcal{B}$ itself being $\lambda$-accessible. Then the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{K}$ is $\lambda$-accessibly embedded. 
\item The inclusion $\mathcal{B} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{K}$  is a $\lambda$-accessible functor in the sense of~\cite[Def.~2.16]{adamek-rosicky}. Here, this just means it preserves $\lambda$-direct limits.
\end{enumerate}
When these conditions hold, we will say that  $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{K}$ is a \textbf{$\boldsymbol{\lambda}$-accessibly embedded, $\boldsymbol{\lambda}$-accessible subcategory}.  We say that $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{K}$  is an \textbf{accessibly embedded, accessible subcategory} if  such  a regular $\lambda$ exists.

\label{lemma-raising-acc-index}
Let $\lambda$ be a regular cardinal.  Assume  that  $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{K}$ is a $\lambda$-accessibly embedded, $\lambda$-accessible subcategory.
\begin{enumerate}
\item  For all regular cardinals  $\lambda' \vartriangleright \lambda$, we again have that  $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{K}$ is a $\lambda'$-accessibly embedded, $\lambda'$-accessible subcategory.
\item  In addition, there exist (arbitrarily large)  regular cardinals  $\mu \vartriangleright \lambda$ such that the $\mu$-presentable objects internal to  the accessible category $\mathcal{B}$ are precisely the objects of $\mathcal{B}$ that are $\mu$-presentable in the ambient accessible category $\mathcal{K}$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{them-rightperp-accessible}
Assume $\mathcal{K}$ is an accessible  additive category with coproducts and   $(\mathcal{K},\mathcal{E})$ is a Quillen exact  structure possessing  a set of admissible generators.
Given any set of objects $\mathcal{S}$,  set  $\mathcal{B} := \mathcal{S}^{\perp}$. That is, $\mathcal{B}$ is the class of all objects $B \in \mathcal{K}$ such that $\operatorname{Ext}_{\mathcal{E}}^1(S,B) = 0$ for all $S\in\mathcal{S}$.  Then  the strictly full subcategory $\mathcal{B}\subseteq \mathcal{K}$ is an accessibly embedded, accessible subcategory.  This implies the existence of (arbitrarily large) regular cardinals $\mu$ such that both of these hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathcal{B}$ and $\mathcal{K}$ are each $\mu$-accessible categories  and the  inclusion functor $\mathcal{B} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{K}$ preserves $\mu$-direct limits.
\item The internal $\mu$-presentable objects of the accessible category $\mathcal{B}$ are precisely the objects of $\mathcal{B}$ that are $\mu$-presentable in the ambient accessible  category $\mathcal{K}$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, $\mathcal{B}$ is closed under $\mu$-direct limits and there is a set  (not a proper class) of $\mu$-presentable objects $T\subseteq \mathcal{B}$ such that each $B\in\mathcal{B}$ is a $\mu$-direct limit of objects from $T$.

\label{cor-rightperp-accessible}
Let $\mathcal{G}$ be any Grothendieck category and let  $(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B})$ be any cotorsion pair that is cogenerated by a set.  Then there exists a regular cardinal $\mu$                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
 such that $\mathcal{B}$ is closed under $\mu$-direct limits, and there is  a set (not a proper class) $T\subseteq \mathcal{B}$ of $\mu$-presentable objects such that each $B\in\mathcal{B}$ is a $\mu$-direct limit of objects from $T$.

\label{def-A-acyclic}
Let $X$ be a chain complex of injective objects from $\mathcal{G}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item  Let $A\in\mathcal{G}$ be an object. We say that $X$ is  \emph{$A$-acyclic} if $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{G}}(A,X)$ is an exact complex of abelian groups.  If $X$ itself is also exact, we say that it is an  \emph{exact $A$-acyclic} complex of injectives.
\item Let $\mathcal{A}\subseteq \mathcal{G}$ be a class of objects. We say that $X$ is \emph{(exact) $\mathcal{A}$-acyclic} if it is (exact) $A$-acyclic for all $A\in\mathcal{A}$.
\end{itemize}

\label{them-A-acyclic-model}
Let $A = \{A_j\} \subseteq \mathcal{G}$ be any set of objects and let   $\mathcal{B}$ be any class of objects  such that $A \subseteq \mathcal{B} \subseteq \varinjlim A$.  Then we have $\mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{B}} = \mathcal{I}_A$ and there is a cofibrantly generated injective   model structure $$\mathfrak{M}_A = (All, \mathcal{W}, \mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{B}})$$ on $\textnormal{Ch}(\mathcal{G})$ with each of the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item  
The thick class $\mathcal{W}$ contains all contractible complexes and  it is  closed under direct limits.
\item An object $M$ of the ground category $\mathcal{G}$ is in $\mathcal{W}$, meaning $S^0(M) \in \mathcal{W}$,  if and only if, $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{G}}(M,X)$ is an exact complex of abelian groups  for all $X \in \mathcal{I}_A$.  In other words, $M$ is trivial precisely   when each  $X\in \mathcal{I}_A$ is $M$-acyclic. \\  In particular, $A \subseteq \mathcal{B} \subseteq \varinjlim A\subseteq \mathcal{W}$.
\item More generally, a  complex  $W$ is  in $\mathcal{W}$, if and only if, the total hom complex $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{G}}(W,X)$ is exact for all $X \in \mathcal{I}_A$.  
\item  $\mathcal{W}$  is also thick when viewed as a full subcategory of $K(\mathcal{G})$, the chain homotopy category of $\mathcal{G}$. A chain map is a weak equivalence if and only if  its mapping cone is in $\mathcal{W}$. The formal homotopy category $\textnormal{Ho}(\mathfrak{M}_A)$  identifies with the Verdier quotient  $K(\mathcal{G})/\mathcal{W}$, and
 it is equivalent to the strictly full subcategory of  $K(\mathcal{G})$ generated  by the complexes in  $\mathcal{I}_A$. Lastly, this is a well-generated triangulated category.
\end{enumerate}

\label{cor-injectives-B-acyclic}
Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a Grothendieck category and $(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B})$ be any cotorsion pair that is cogenerated by a set.   Then there exists a set of objects $T\subseteq \mathcal{B}$ such that
 $\mathcal{I}_T = \mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{B}}$ and for which applying Theorem~\ref{them-A-acyclic-model} to $T$ yields exactly $\mathfrak{M}_T = (All, \mathcal{W}, \mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{B}})$. That is, the fibrant objects are precisely the (not necessarily exact) $\mathcal{B}$-acyclic complexes of injectives.

\label{cor-injectives-acyclic}
Let $\mathcal{I}$ denote the class of all injective objects in any Grothendieck category $\mathcal{G}$.  Allow $\mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{I}}$ to denote  the class of all (not necessarily exact)  $\mathcal{I}$-acyclic complexes of injectives $X$.  Then there exists a set of injective objects $T$ such that
 $\mathcal{I}_T = \mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{I}}$ and for which applying Theorem~\ref{them-A-acyclic-model} to $T$ yields exactly $\mathfrak{M}_T = (All, \mathcal{W}, \mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{I}})$.

\label{def-G-inj-B-tate}
Let $\mathcal{B}\subseteq \mathcal{G}$ be any class of objects.                                                                
  We remind the reader of some notation set in  Section~\ref{section-finite-dim}:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\textnormal{\footnotesize{ex}}{\mathcal{I}}_{\mathcal{B}}$ denotes the class of all exact $\mathcal{B}$-acyclic complexes of injectives.                                                                                         
\item $\mathcal{W} := {}^\perp \textnormal{\footnotesize{ex}}{\mathcal{I}}_{\mathcal{B}}$ is the left $\operatorname{Ext}$ orthogonal in $\textnormal{Ch}(\mathcal{G})$.
\end{itemize}
Now, we set the following definitions and notation:
\begin{itemize}
\item  An object $N \in \mathcal{G}$ is called \emph{Gorenstein $\mathcal{B}$-injective} if it is equal to the 0-cycle, $N=Z_0X$, of some exact $\mathcal{B}$-acyclic complex of injectives  $X \in \textnormal{\footnotesize{ex}}{\mathcal{I}}_{\mathcal{B}}$.
\item  $\mathcal{GI}_{\mathcal{B}}$ denotes the class of all Gorenstein $\mathcal{B}$-injective objects.
\item  An object $M\in\mathcal{G}$ will be called \emph{$\mathcal{B}$-Tate trivial} if $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{G}}(M,X)$ is an exact complex of abelian groups for all $X\in\textnormal{\footnotesize{ex}}{\mathcal{I}}_{\mathcal{B}}$.
\end{itemize}

\label{definition-B Tate trivial-generators}
Let $\mathcal{B}\subseteq \mathcal{G}$ be a class of objects.
If $\mathcal{U}$  is a generating set for $\mathcal{G}$, and $\mathcal{U}\subseteq \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{G}}  \, (= {}^\perp \mathcal{GI}_{\mathcal{B}}$), then we will call it a \emph{set of $\mathcal{B}$-Tate trivial generators}. In the special  case  of when $\mathcal{B}$ is the class of all injectives we just call $\mathcal{U}$ a set of \emph{Tate trivial generators}.

\label{lemma-generators-cond}
Let $\mathcal{B}\subseteq \mathcal{G}$ be a class of objects. The following statements are equivalent for a given set $\mathcal{U}$ of generators for $\mathcal{G}$. Moreover, the existence of such a generating set is necessary if $({}^\perp \mathcal{GI}_{\mathcal{B}}, \mathcal{GI}_{\mathcal{B}})$ is to be a complete cotorsion pair.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathcal{U}$ is a set of $\mathcal{B}$-Tate trivial generators. 
\item Each $X \in  \textnormal{\footnotesize{ex}}{\mathcal{I}}_{\mathcal{B}}$ is $\mathcal{U}$-acyclic.
\end{enumerate}
Finally, we note that these conditions are automatically satisfied (for any class $\mathcal{B}$)
whenever $\mathcal{U}$ is a set of generators of finite projective dimension.

\label{them-Goren-inj-cot}
Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a  Grothendieck category and $(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B})$ be a cotorsion pair that is cogenerated by a set. Then
 $({}^\perp \mathcal{GI}_{\mathcal{B}}, \mathcal{GI}_{\mathcal{B}})$ is a complete cotorsion pair if and only if  $\mathcal{G}$ admits a set $\mathcal{U}$  of $\mathcal{B}$-Tate trivial generators. 
 In this case,
 $(\mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{G}}, \mathcal{GI}_{\mathcal{B}})$ is an injective  cotorsion pair cogenerated by a set. We call it the \textbf{Gorenstein $\mathcal{B}$-injective cotorsion pair}. In particular, $\mathfrak{M} = (All, \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{G}}, \mathcal{GI}_{\mathcal{B}})$  represents a  cofibrantly generated and injective abelian model structure on $\mathcal{G}$. We call it  the \textbf{Gorenstein $\mathcal{B}$-injective model structure} and it satisfies these properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\textnormal{Ho}(\mathfrak{M})$ is well-generated, and the kernel of the triangulated localization functor $\gamma \colon \mathcal{G} \xrightarrow{} \textnormal{Ho}(\mathfrak{M})$ contains $\mathcal{U}$ and $\mathcal{B}$ and is closed under direct limits.
\item For any other injective model structure $(All, \mathcal{V}, \mathcal{F})$ with $\mathcal{B}\subseteq \mathcal{V}$, we must have $\mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{G}}\subseteq \mathcal{V}$, equivalently, $\mathcal{F}\subseteq \mathcal{GI}_{\mathcal{B}}$. 
\item The sphere functor $S^0(-) \colon \mathcal{G} \xrightarrow{} \textnormal{Ch}(\mathcal{G})$ is a (left) Quillen equivalence from the Gorenstein $\mathcal{B}$-injective model structure  to the exact $\mathcal{B}$-acyclic model structure of  Corollary~\ref{cor-exact-injectives-B-acyclic}. (See Proposition~\ref{prop-quillen-equivalence}.)
\end{enumerate}

\label{cor-Goren-inj}
For any Grothendieck category $\mathcal{G}$,  we have  that $({}^\perp \mathcal{GI}, \mathcal{GI})$ is a complete cotorsion pair and enjoys all of the properties listed in Theorem~\ref{them-Goren-inj-cot} if and only if $\mathcal{G}$ admits a set of Tate trivial generators.
In particular, this is the case for  any locally finite dimensional Grothendieck category $\mathcal{G}$.

\label{prop-quillen-equivalence}
The sphere functor $S^0(-) \colon \mathcal{G} \xrightarrow{} \textnormal{Ch}(\mathcal{G})$ is a (left) Quillen equivalence from the Gorenstein $\mathcal{B}$-injective model structure of Theorem~\ref{them-Goren-inj-cot} to the exact $\mathcal{B}$-acyclic model structure of  Corollary~\ref{cor-exact-injectives-B-acyclic}. Its right adjoint (Quillen inverse) is given by the 0-cycles functor, $Z_0(-)$.
Therefore, the associated total derived adjunction is a triangulated equivalence between their homotopy categories.
